5417] [17592186112059 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112059 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bcaf7fef-e1a9-5f92-8805-44dff0fff56f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112060 #edb/ref 1080 "images/56/56f73c166e5be1be3536d363eaec01acdba4ac4ae5c4027d1a9e8254309fbce0.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112060 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112060 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2692ad15-4743-5b90-813d-3cf394dbe3f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112061 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2b/2b78032ffd515ab81928a4d639b83783bdea3a8a79eeb7300c6b89a8e9bcb63c.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "45b974c5-21c7-583e-9f09-99a5331f688e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112062 #edb/ref 1080 "images/5e/5e2bf6ccf5f5725ac82f176300e3c5a2d20cc7270ca33e1f28c9e99c5cad18d2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112062 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112062 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4adf586b-3455-5f58-810d-5d58de6b52e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115088 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115088 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph $y=f(x+2)$ for $y=f(x)$ given below.\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e320a97a-d524-5225-895e-76034e599fa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115088 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x+2)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left*. So, every point on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$ is two units *to the left* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/75/758899a1abd5298429059016a4127db61eda014e8c46e0539e9226c5641e5ceb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115089 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115089 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2,0)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x-7).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dec57af-1c02-56fe-b38e-e62795fdc93d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115089 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x-7)$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $7$ units to the *right*. So the $y$-coordinate remains unchanged while the $x$-coordinate increases by $7.$\n\nWe know that the point $(-2,0)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(-2+7,0) = (5,0)$ must lie on $y=f(x-7).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115090 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115090 #edb/ref 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=f(x+1)-2$ for $y=f(x)$ given below.\n\n![](images/38/3814318e7d04bf8457c0e5a9a5d563412bb6a988eda83fca84b8400ffea2a117.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86b96055-a0e1-5dc7-9f89-364f77412a77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115090 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph $y=f(x+1)-2,$ we translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the left and then $2$ units down.\n\n![](images/17/1744139728266d53c546e0b98452e042b8d099cebed12f5ee0a7c230257e9fa2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115103 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115103 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) .$ What point must lie on $y=f(x + 1)+5?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "740d5deb-3982-5293-9071-ddaaa189fb81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115103 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x+1)+5$  is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *left* and $5$ units up. So the $y$-coordinate increases by $5$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $1.$\n\nWe know that the point $P(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-1,0+5) = (0,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x+1)+5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting a Horizontal Translation of a Given Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115259 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "372e7248-7fe0-5138-896d-61b4866f83cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115260 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "919da198-25e0-5b6f-8d99-79f1508759b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1039 "Plotting Horizontal and Vertical Translations of a Given Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115261 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d7c84dd5-2c76-5584-81a1-53d2459afb94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally and Vertically Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115274 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "57ef1e2c-07d4-5d04-9de8-8884cae17c00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1045 "Horizontal Translations of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115418 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "abceb987-e559-5c80-8a21-441171253b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186112045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115506 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/5573133cd1a199d53254947f951999a10731d980faffcf75e25ac6c3e66cd861.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "288404cd-b53d-5960-beb2-b80beaaa484f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115506 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x-1)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *right*. So, every point on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$ is one unit *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/96/96e78d86fc113988887c0c35daf2ebf119f2f47be8d82822d439b2f47433c31d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186112057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115508 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f3/f35440c0132f7e98617f2d700b60563d44b7e9cb3af3850f557d4b169cc08bb7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x-3)+2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1050c6d7-bf6b-5e55-8ad0-841edc477272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115508 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph $y=f(x-3)+2,$ we translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units to the right and then $2$ units up.\n\n![](images/3c/3cdbb6ef28bc47734820fadaef1e65061775b8c96724713f7f8de30e8a5d0d5e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1061 18017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6345469f-1236-5eaf-8f5f-c51d47bf4f3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116148 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1061 18018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d171b522-79a1-5dbc-bcf1-aa7179ecd080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116149 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1061 18019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5c16920-e146-5ef0-841d-e2645a4e1de6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116150 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1061 18020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6cc0af1-c408-599c-ac0a-69b7af985fac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116151 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1061 18022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b18fd01e-e160-5b17-a9cf-730b2b261357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116174 #edb/ref 1061 19261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116174 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cdd1f56-3cc3-5455-896a-aec5448b7269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116174 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, for $k>0,$\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2).$ \n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4).$ The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that $f(3)=-4.$ Then if $x={\\color{red}5},$ we have \n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4,\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ \n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math]} \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1,2) & (3,2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (2,1.5) \\\\ (-2,0) & (0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1065 3280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da231b95-be14-58c3-a8fe-6261d61c7d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116503 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1064 "When graphing a function, we can combine vertical and horizontal translations. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x-h)+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $h$ units to the right and $k$ units up.\n\nIt doesn't matter which translation we do first, but it's often easiest to start with the horizontal translation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1065 3282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ceb4fcff-ae84-5217-aa0c-16d64fc23eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116504 #edb/ref 1067 "Combining Vertical and Horizontal Translations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524387 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524387 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524387 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04ea9c31-066d-5652-a164-4fc1fda7f5d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524405 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524405 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524405 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d64ca17-c947-58ed-8484-2b4faddc327b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524424 #edb/r